# [[ .Name ]]

A quan. It cannot run on its own: it has no `main`. A quanar builds it into its own program
with the quanar's host, runs it in its own process and does its work. Everything it needs
from the outside comes through its qua.
[[ if not .Quas ]]
> **No qua yet.** Add a qua to the project's `go.work`, and use its `api` and `db` in `Mount`.
[[ end ]]
- `quan.go`: `Mount` asks the quanar to listen: `api.Get("/[[ .Name ]]", …)`, `api.Post("/[[ .Name ]]", …)`.
  Every path starts with the quan's name.
- `handlers.go` take a request and return a response; they only call services.
- `services.go` hold the quan's rules and use the qua's `db`, naming the table themselves.

```sh
go tool quan-build --watch    # builds, serves the program on port [[ .Port ]] and announces each new build at /events
```

`quan-build` is the quanar's tool, found through the project's `go.work`. It links the quanar's
host, which holds `main`, with this package. The quanar follows `/events`: it loads the quan
when the stream opens, reloads it on every new build and unmounts it when the stream closes.

If the workspace holds more than one quanar, name the one to build with:
`go tool <quanar>/cmd/quan-build --watch`.
